Ladybird retains inline border-box rectangles after a legacy line clamp
for offset dimensions and client-rectangle queries. These rectangles
follow relative positioning and writing-mode conversion, while painting,
hit testing, and the clamped container size use the visible lines.

Blink’s InlineLayoutAlgorithm::CreateLine() retains hidden line items.
LayoutInline::CollectLineBoxRects() and QuadsForSelfInternal() enumerate
their rectangles without filtering the is_hidden_due_to_layout flag.

Gecko keeps inline continuations beyond the clamp point. Its painting
code in nsBlockFrame::BuildDisplayList() skips the suppressed lines.
Geometry queries use nsLayoutUtils::GetAllInFlowRects(), which visits
every continuation through GetAllInFlowBoxes(), including hidden ones.

WebKit retains legacy-clamped lines in its inline layout. The method
InlineFormattingContext::createDisplayContentForInlineContent() marks
lines past the limit as fully truncated in the block direction, while
InlineFormattingUtils::shouldDiscardRemainingContentInBlockDirection()
only discards non-legacy-clamped content. Ladybird matches the engines’
separation between retained geometry and suppressed painting.
